# Plan → Grill gate

When designing a plan (Plan mode or an explicit planning request):

## After a RepoLens audit (`run_complete`)

Do **not** offer — **run** `batch-grilling` on the open finding decisions (frontier rounds, option A = Best Practice). That is the default wrap-up, not an optional design grill.

## Offer one-at-a-time grilling when ANY of these hold (non-audit plans)

- Multiple modules / libraries / workflows change
- Irreversible choice (public API, data format, agent policy, release channel)
- New abstraction or process that will outlive this chat
- User is unsure between two+ approaches

Then: say you recommend `/grill-me` (or the `grilling` skill), ask one clarifying question if needed, and wait. Do not implement until they confirm the plan or decline grilling.

If the non-audit plan already has **many independent** open decisions (>~3), prefer offering `/batch-grill-me` instead of one-at-a-time.

## Skip grilling when

- Single-file / obvious bugfix
- CI green-up, typo, or mechanical retarget
- User already gave a locked decision and said to execute

Do **not** auto-start a full one-at-a-time grill on every ordinary plan — offer it, then proceed only after confirmation or an explicit “skip grill / just plan”. Audit wrap-up is the exception (batch-grilling is mandatory).
